      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Understanding the Rejection</h2>
        <p>A visa rejection is painful, but it's not the end. Many rejected applicants successfully reapply or appeal. The first step is understanding why your application was denied. Your rejection letter should state the reason—carefully review it, as it determines your next move.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Common rejection reasons fall into categories: (1) Document issues, (2) Income problems, (3) Background concerns, (4) Procedural errors, (5) Fraud suspicions, (6) Health/security issues. Understanding which category your rejection falls into helps you decide whether to appeal or reapply.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Top 10 Rejection Reasons (and What to Do About Each)</h2>

      <h3>1. Insufficient Income Documentation</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> Your bank statements don't clearly show the required monthly income, deposits are inconsistent, or the income source is unclear.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Gather 24 months of statements instead of 12. Include letters from your bank confirming your average monthly deposits. Get employer/client letters confirming they pay you. Provide detailed explanation of income source. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Reapply—this is a document quality issue fixable with better documentation.</p>

      <h3>2. Income Below Minimum Threshold</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> Your documented income is genuinely below the required amount (€2,316/month for NLV, €2,160 for DNV).</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> If you genuinely don't have enough income, you're not currently eligible. Wait until your income increases, or consider a different visa category. If you actually have enough income but it wasn't documented, reapply with better documentation. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> If income is truly insufficient, don't reapply yet. If it's a documentation issue, reapply with clearer proof.</p>

      <h3>3. Poor Document Quality or Incomplete Application</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> Documents are illegible, scans are pixelated, pages are missing, or your application was missing entire sections.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> This is easily fixable. Scan documents at 300 DPI minimum. Ensure all documents are in order, numbered, and organized. Include a checklist showing every required item. Have someone else review your application for completeness. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Reapply—appeals won't help with document quality issues.</p>

      <h3>4. Health Insurance Not Recognized (DNV)</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> Your insurance plan wasn't on the consulate's approved list, or it has copayments when the visa requires "sin copago."</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Contact the consulate and ask which insurers they accept. Get a new policy from an approved provider with explicit "no copay" clause. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Reapply with correct insurance. No point appealing this—the fix is straightforward.</p>

      <h3>5. Background Check Issues</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> You have criminal history, outstanding warrants, immigration violations (past overstay), or fraud concerns.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Get updated police clearance from your country. If you have a criminal record, consider consulting an immigration lawyer before reapplying—some issues make you ineligible. If it's a past overstay, show evidence it's been resolved. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Consult a lawyer. Some background issues are appealing; others require a lawyer for reapplication strategy.</p>

      <h3>6. Unclear Ties to Home Country</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> The consulate suspects you're trying to immigrate (relocation) rather than obtain residency. You provided no evidence you'd return to your home country.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Include evidence of home country ties: property ownership, family in your home country, ongoing business/employment, professional licenses/certifications still valid, rental property. Show you have reasons to return home periodically. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Reapply with strong ties documentation.</p>

      <h3>7. Inconsistent Income Sources</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> Your bank statements show sporadic deposits that don't align with your claimed income sources. Deposits are irregular or don't match your story.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Provide detailed explanation (e.g., "Income deposited quarterly from my rental properties, not monthly"). Include supporting letters from income sources confirming payment schedule. Show the pattern across 24 months. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Reapply with clarification letters from income sources.</p>

      <h3>8. Missing Supporting Documentation</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> You forgot to include required documents: pension letters, investment account statements, medical certificate (if required), or employment history.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Use a detailed checklist from the consulate. Physically check each item off. Include one extra document of each type (e.g., two pension letters instead of one). <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Reapply—this is entirely fixable.</p>

      <h3>9. Fraud or Misrepresentation Suspicions</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> The consulate suspects your documents are fake, you've lied about your circumstances, or your identity is unclear.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> This is serious. Consult an immigration lawyer. You may face bans. A lawyer can help explain concerns and gather evidence of legitimacy. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Appeal with legal representation, or consult lawyer before attempting reapplication.</p>

      <h3>10. Administrative/Procedural Errors</h3>
      <p><strong>Why it happens:</strong> The consulate made a mistake in processing your application: lost documents, miscalculated income, wrong decision sent.</p>
      <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Request clarification from the consulate. If there's a clear procedural error, appeal immediately. <strong>Reapply or appeal?</strong> Appeal—procedural errors are grounds for successful appeals.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Critical:</strong> Some rejections can result in re-entry bans or make you ineligible for future visas. Before reapplying or appealing, understand if your rejection puts you at future risk. Consult an immigration lawyer if there's any mention of fraud or violation.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Should You Appeal or Reapply?</h2>

      <p><strong>Appeal if:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>You believe the decision is procedurally wrong (e.g., consulate misread your documents, made a calculation error).</li>
        <li>New evidence has emerged that wasn't available during your original application.</li>
        <li>You can identify a specific legal error in the consulate's reasoning.</li>
        <li>You believe the consulate acted arbitrarily or didn't follow their own guidelines.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Reapply if:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your documents were incomplete or poor quality—you can fix this.</li>
        <li>You didn't document your income clearly—you can improve documentation.</li>
        <li>Your circumstances have changed for the better since the rejection (e.g., your income has increased).</li>
        <li>You've addressed the core issue that caused the rejection.</li>
        <li>The rejection was based on something easily fixable (insurance, health certificate, etc.).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">The Appeal Process (Recurso de Reposición)</h2>

      <p>Spain's primary appeal mechanism for visa rejections is the "recurso de reposición" (administrative reexamination). Here's how it works:</p>

      <p><strong>Step 1: Deadline</strong> You have 30 days from receiving your rejection letter to file an appeal. This deadline is strict—missing it means you lose your right to appeal at this level.</p>

      <p><strong>Step 2: Grounds for Appeal</strong> Your appeal must state specific grounds: procedural error, new evidence, legal misapplication, or arbitrary decision-making. Generic "please reconsider" appeals are rejected.</p>

      <p><strong>Step 3: Submission</strong> Submit your appeal to the same consulate that issued your rejection. Include: original rejection letter, detailed written explanation (preferably 2–3 pages), specific grounds for appeal, and any new supporting documents.</p>

      <p><strong>Step 4: Review Process</strong> The consulate re-examines your case. This typically takes 6–12 weeks. The original decision-maker may reconsider, or a superior reviews the case.</p>

      <p><strong>Step 5: Appeal Decision</strong> You'll receive written notification of the appeal outcome. Most appeals are denied; successful appeals usually involved clear procedural errors.</p>

      <p><strong>Step 6: Further Appeal (if needed)</strong> If your recurso de reposición is denied, you can file a "recurso de alzada" (higher-level appeal) with the Spanish Ministry of Inclusion. This is more formal and often requires legal representation.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">💡</span>
        <p><strong>Reality check:</strong> Most appeals fail. Consulates are resistant to overturning their own decisions. Appeals work best when there's a clear procedural error. For most rejections, reapplication with improved documentation succeeds faster than appealing.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Strengthening Your Reapplication</h2>

      <p><strong>1. Address the Stated Reason Directly</strong> Your rejection letter identified a specific issue. Make that issue impossible to question in your reapplication. If it was income documentation, provide triple the evidence. If it was document quality, provide pristine scans.</p>

      <p><strong>2. Expand Your Documentation Package</strong> Don't just resubmit what you had before. Add: (1) 24 months of bank statements instead of 12, (2) Written explanation of your income sources, (3) Letters from employers/clients confirming income, (4) Bank letters verifying your account in good standing, (5) Professional recommendation letters, (6) Detailed personal statement explaining your circumstances.</p>

      <p><strong>3. Use Professional Help</strong> A visa specialist or lawyer can identify subtle issues in your original application you might not see. They often achieve reapproval when individuals don't. Cost is €300–€1,500 but often worth it.</p>

      <p><strong>4. Allow Time Between Applications</strong> Don't reapply immediately. Wait 30–60 days. Use this time to strengthen your file, address the rejection reason, and potentially improve your circumstances. Immediate reapplication with the same weak materials will fail again.</p>

      <p><strong>5. Request Pre-Approval for Key Items</strong> Before reapplying, contact the consulate and ask them to pre-approve your insurance (if DNV), health certificate format, or other items that caused issues. Get written confirmation they're acceptable.</p>

      <p><strong>6. Frame Your Cover Letter Carefully</strong> Include a brief (1 page) explanation addressing the rejection reason. Don't be defensive. Clearly explain how you've addressed the issue. "In my original application, income documentation was unclear. In this reapplication, I provide 24 months of statements plus letters from my financial institutions clarifying the monthly average of €X,XXX."</p>

      <p><strong>7. Double-Check Everything</strong> Have someone else review your application before submission. Check: document organization, completeness (use a checklist), scans readability, signatures/dates, consistency across documents.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">What to Do While Waiting for an Appeal Decision</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Begin strengthening your documentation:</strong> Start gathering documents for a potential reapplication immediately.</li>
        <li><strong>Address the core issue:</strong> If your income was questioned, work on increasing it or finding better documentation. If background was an issue, explore options for resolution.</li>
        <li><strong>Consult a lawyer:</strong> If your rejection mentions fraud, background issues, or legal problems, get professional legal advice immediately.</li>
        <li><strong>Don't travel to Spain:</strong> While your visa is rejected, entering Spain could result in complications.</li>
        <li><strong>Monitor your email:</strong> The consulate may request additional information. Respond immediately to any contact.</li>
        <li><strong>Continue building your case:</strong> Every day of delayed application is another day you can document your stability and circumstances.</li>
      </ul>
